The `interpolateRoutePath` function can crash if `params` is explicitly passed as `null` (since the default parameter `params = {}` only applies when `params` is `undefined`). Additionally, path resolution helpers should handle non-string or null/undefined inputs gracefully by returning `null` instead of throwing an error, allowing the application to fail closed. All path components should be validated as non-empty strings, returning `null` if any required component is missing or invalid.

```javascript
export function interpolateRoutePath(routeId, params) {
  const route = getRouteById(routeId);
  if (!route || typeof route.path !== "string") {
    return null;
  }
  const safeParams = params || {};
  let isInvalid = false;
  const path = route.path.replace(/:([A-Za-z0-9_]+)/g, function(_match, paramName) {
    const value = safeParams[paramName];
    if (value === null || typeof value === "undefined") {
      isInvalid = true;
      return "";
    }
    const stringValue = String(value).trim();
    if (stringValue === "") {
      isInvalid = true;
      return "";
    }
    return encodeURIComponent(stringValue);
  });
  return isInvalid ? null : path;
}
```

